WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.039
So across the pond, spaz

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.109
is short for spastic.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.549
The spaz that I grew up with as a

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.109
teenager in the 90 seconds, fans

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.849
know. Do you want to by being like a

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.949
total laundry spaz?

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.389
He's a spazz. 

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:13.289
Where it meant that you were, like, maybe a little bit

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.489
random. It could even be a term of

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.490
endearment.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.299
That is

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.249
not the same as the space that's

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:22.319
used in African-American vernacular

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:23.669
English, where it basically means

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.469
that you're about to fight somebody.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.569
So we don't use that term in the US

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.669
the way that they do in

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:32.008
the UK, which is where

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.559
this shortening to spaz has become

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.119
the equivalent of a slur.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:38.069
Probably the closest analog

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.959
in American English is like

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.239
retard. It carries that type

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.019
of weight.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.089
In early summer 2022, Lizzo

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:46.499
was called out by an Australian

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.509
activist for using the word spaz

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.510
and her new song Girlzz.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:51.419
Lizzo made sure to emphasize that

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.429
she herself as a fat black woman

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.619
in America, had experienced

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.509
oppression and language

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.559
based oppression.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.229
She did sort of remind

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.329
this activist from Australia

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.339
which side her progressive bread

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.239
was buttered on.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.099
I've had many hurtful words used

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.689
against me, so I understand the

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.609
power words can have, Lizzo

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.929
said in a statement posted to

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.099
Instagram.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.509
I'm proud to say there's a new

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.249
version of Girlzz with the lyric

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.649
change. Hold my bag.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.419
Do you see the shit?

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.420
Hold me back.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.949
Just seven weeks later, Beyoncé

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.500
operated for using a similar phrase,

00:01:27.501 --> 00:01:29.699
"spazzing on that ass,"

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:30.959
in her song Heated.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.119
Fans never heard from Beyoncé

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.919
herself because Lizzo sort of primed

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:39.309
to pump for her.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:40.829
Beyoncé didn't, I think, really have

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:42.989
much of a choice to be responsive

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:44.879
in the way that she was.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.519
Two major artists

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.649
altered the lyrics to their songs.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.569
They arrest the word spaz

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.489
in post-production after the album's

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.749
already been released, after

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.329
activists from Australia

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.189
and other locations across the pond

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.439
espouses a much worse word

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.509
than it is in the US, objected

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.429
and said that it was ablest.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:07.169
This is a rapidly advancing new

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.939
frontier for the suppression of free

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.008
speech and artistic expression,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:12.719
Rosenfield writes in a new article

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:13.979
for Reason magazine.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.929
Stop spazzing out about Spaz.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:17.669
It's something beyond burning books,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.529
something beyond destruction.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.449
It's stories and songs and films

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.399
cut apart and written over

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.439
leaving no trace and no remnant

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.999
of what ever used to be.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.099
It wasn't unheard of

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:30.839
for an artist to alter their lyrics

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:32.159
after the fact. You could expect

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.109
them to respond to you that they

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.479
were sort of a moral authority.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.369
This idea that you needed to be able

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:40.109
to look up to artists not just

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.669
because you enjoyed the art they

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.289
made, but because they were supposed

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.209
to represent some kind of a role

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.099
model to you

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.119
as the listener.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.139
They're supposed to reflect your

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.729
values, you know, morally,

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:51.959
politically, etc..

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.119
I think that people

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.009
got not just used

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.989
to, but maybe a little bit drunk

00:02:57.990 --> 00:02:59.939
on the realization that

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:01.769
if they made enough noise,

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.749
they could kind of emotionally

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.729
blackmail artists

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.199
who were concerned with reputation

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.329
management into

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:10.409
doing their bidding.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.569
On the one hand, people who

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.519
like music or like

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.439
books, who like to consume

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.329
culture, and on the other hand,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.339
you have people who want

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.199
to control culture, who want to

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.549
go back to the artist and say, I

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.989
don't like what you did here.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:26.459
It offends me. Change it.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.559
There is virtually no overlap

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.849
between these two groups.

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.679
They have completely separate sets

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.209
of motivations, completely separate

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:34.529
things that they're trying to

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.939
achieve when they engage with a

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:36.569
given work.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.429
Artists are left at the nexus

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:40.109
of this where they want to be

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.629
creating work for the former people,

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:43.889
people who genuinely like art,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.229
but they are subject

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.179
to the whims and

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.769
the punishments, the consequences

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.479
meted out by the latter group.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.639
And I don't think that

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.499
this is a great environment in

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.449
which to be trying to

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:59.339
create much of anything, let alone

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.809
create it bravely.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:02.729
Swift's new video for her song

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.159
Anti-Hero is facing controversy

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.379
over a song where she steps

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.209
on a scale that reads fat.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:10.979
Critics are saying she's promoting

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:12.719
so-called fatphobia, but her

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.699
supporters are pointing out how

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.078
she has always been open about her

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:18.208
struggle with her own eating

00:04:18.209 --> 00:04:20.309
disorders. And the video reflects

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.319
what she sees, not what

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.429
you see her seeing.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:24.989
Taylor Swift chose to alter the

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.429
scene in her music video, where she

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:27.929
is standing on a scale that reads

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:29.909
fat to her, just simply

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.379
staring at her disruptive and

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.179
disapproving doppelganger.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.559
This actually strikes me as maybe

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.539
the most tragic example of

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.789
this happening on a contemporary

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:40.979
landscape, because here

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.259
is a case of not just

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.239
a word being used in a context

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:46.649
by one person where another person

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:47.969
says, Well, in our culture, that's

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.159
offensive. This is a woman talking

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.529
about her own personal history,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.539
revealing something that is painful

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.039
about herself and exploring that

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.959
through her art and the idea that

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.099
she is going to be scolded.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.959
And made to change

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:03.789
her work because

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.349
other people didn't like the way

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:06.849
that she was negotiating her own

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:08.739
pain in her artwork.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.169
That just strikes me as wildly

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.339
inappropriate.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:15.009
By being able to pull back

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.139
your original work and change

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.179
it in real time so that

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.039
the digital copy that gets released

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.269
on streaming services, which is the

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:23.769
way that most people now consume

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:25.839
music, is not just different,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.849
but it seems like it's always been

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:28.569
that way.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.639
It's basically creating a second

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.739
reality.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.419
There's a sense now that maybe

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:35.799
nothing is permanent.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.269
Art isn't going to be permanent

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.609
anymore, that it will

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:42.549
update itself in accordance with

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:44.109
contemporary sensibilities.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.669
So if something in it becomes

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:47.619
offensive, well, we

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:48.999
don't have to reckon with that as an

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.889
artifact of an earlier time anymore.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.229
It is insidious

00:05:53.230 --> 00:05:55.149
that this is happening

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:57.009
in such a hidden way.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.559
There's no bonfire of books.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.329
If somebody like Beyoncé does not

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.219
take a stand on her right to create

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.929
as she pleases, there's very, very

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.279
little hope for anybody else,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.719
including artists who are just

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.939
emerging, who are just developing

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:11.589
and trying to take risks but can't

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:12.590
or are too afraid to.

